<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-k"><span class="sn">03</span>The old website</div><h2 class="rv">What customers actually saw</h2><p class="lede rv">These pages come from the Internet Archive’s Wayback Machine. They are live views loaded from the Wayback Machine with scripts disabled. Captures were found with the CDX API in October 2026. Archived pages from 1999–2000 are often incomplete, and we note where a render is broken.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-36">36</a></sup></p></div>
<div class="wb-strip rv" id="wbStrip"><button class="on" data-i="0"><b>May 1999</b>Homepage, WebMagic era</button><button class="" data-i="1"><b>May 1999</b>About Pets.com</button><button class="" data-i="2"><b>Nov 1999</b>Holiday homepage</button><button class="" data-i="3"><b>Feb 2000</b>Anniversary sale</button><button class="" data-i="4"><b>Mar 2000</b>Sock puppet gear</button><button class="" data-i="5"><b>Mar 2000</b>The dog store</button><button class="" data-i="6"><b>May 2000</b>Homepage, editorial co</button><button class="" data-i="7"><b>Jun 2000</b>Product page</button><button class="" data-i="8"><b>Nov 10, 2000</b>The farewell letter</button><button class="" data-i="9"><b>Feb 2001</b>The new owner</button></div>
<div id="wbViews"><div class="wb-view" data-i="0">
<figure class="browser"><div class="chrome"><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="url">http://www2.pets.com/</span></div><div class="shot live"><div class="ld">Loading the archived page from the Internet Archive…</div><iframe data-src="https://web.archive.org/web/19990508060549if_/http://www2.pets.com:80/" title="Archived http://www2.pets.com/ captured May 8, 1999 · 06:05 UTC" sandbox="allow-same-origin" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"></iframe></div></figure>
<figcaption class="wb-cap"><div class="d">Captured May 8, 1999 · 06:05 UTC</div><h3>Homepage, WebMagic era</h3><p>Early www2.pets.com storefront, before the Amazon-backed relaunch. Navigation offered Dog, Cat, Fish, Bird, Small Pet and Ferret products plus a Community and a Knowledge Center.</p><div class="warn">Note: Most images were never archived. The capture renders as layout with blank image boxes.</div><div class="arch"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/19990508060549/http://www2.pets.com:80/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View this capture on the Wayback Machine ↗</a></div></figcaption>
</div><div class="wb-view" data-i="1" hidden>
<figure class="browser"><div class="chrome"><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="url">http://www2.pets.com/aboutus.cfm</span></div><div class="shot live"><div class="ld">Loading the archived page from the Internet Archive…</div><iframe data-src="https://web.archive.org/web/19990508063132if_/http://www2.pets.com:80/aboutus.cfm" title="Archived http://www2.pets.com/aboutus.cfm captured May 8, 1999 · 06:31 UTC" sandbox="allow-same-origin" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"></iframe></div></figure>
<figcaption class="wb-cap"><div class="d">Captured May 8, 1999 · 06:31 UTC</div><h3>“About Pets.com”</h3><p>The About page still called the site “a division of WebMagic™”, the company led by founder Greg McLemore. Pets.com bought the domain and store on Feb 17, 1999.</p><div class="arch"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/19990508063132/http://www2.pets.com:80/aboutus.cfm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View this capture on the Wayback Machine ↗</a></div></figcaption>
</div><div class="wb-view" data-i="2" hidden>
<figure class="browser"><div class="chrome"><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="url">http://www.pets.com/cgi-bin/puppy/home/home.jsp?BV_UseBVCookie=YES&animal=Home</span></div><div class="shot live"><div class="ld">Loading the archived page from the Internet Archive…</div><iframe data-src="https://web.archive.org/web/19991128201851if_/http://www.pets.com:80/cgi-bin/puppy/home/home.jsp?BV_UseBVCookie=YES&animal=Home" title="Archived http://www.pets.com/cgi-bin/puppy/home/home.jsp?BV_UseBVCookie=YES&animal=Home captured Nov 28, 1999 · 20:18 UTC" sandbox="allow-same-origin" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"></iframe></div></figure>
<figcaption class="wb-cap"><div class="d">Captured Nov 28, 1999 · 20:18 UTC</div><h3>Holiday homepage</h3><p>Thanksgiving weekend 1999, two months before the Super Bowl: a lime-green header, a black tab bar and a pale-yellow sidebar. The text advertises holiday gift baskets and sale prices on dog food.</p><div class="warn">Note: When an image is missing, Wayback substitutes the nearest-dated copy it has. Some banners in this render, such as the “10% off” anniversary art, probably come from early 2000 captures.</div><div class="arch"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/19991128201851/http://www.pets.com:80/cgi-bin/puppy/home/home.jsp?BV_UseBVCookie=YES&animal=Home" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View this capture on the Wayback Machine ↗</a></div></figcaption>
</div><div class="wb-view" data-i="3" hidden>
<figure class="browser"><div class="chrome"><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="url">http://www.pets.com/cgi-bin/puppy/home/home.jsp?BV_UseBVCookie=YES&sourceid=00093048810421705111</span></div><div class="shot live"><div class="ld">Loading the archived page from the Internet Archive…</div><iframe data-src="https://web.archive.org/web/20000229050138if_/http://www.pets.com:80/cgi-bin/puppy/home/home.jsp?BV_UseBVCookie=YES&sourceid=00093048810421705111" title="Archived http://www.pets.com/cgi-bin/puppy/home/home.jsp?BV_UseBVCookie=YES&sourceid=00093048810421705111 captured Feb 29, 2000 · 05:01 UTC" sandbox="allow-same-origin" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"></iframe></div></figure>
<figcaption class="wb-cap"><div class="d">Captured Feb 29, 2000 · 05:01 UTC</div><h3>Anniversary sale</h3><p>Eighteen days after the IPO: “Celebrate our Anniversary with 10% off Everything.” The sock puppet appears in the right column.</p><div class="arch"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20000229050138/http://www.pets.com:80/cgi-bin/puppy/home/home.jsp?BV_UseBVCookie=YES&sourceid=00093048810421705111" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View this capture on the Wayback Machine ↗</a></div></figcaption>
</div><div class="wb-view" data-i="4" hidden>
<figure class="browser"><div class="chrome"><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="url">http://www.pets.com/cgi-bin/puppy/products/category_home.jsp?BV_UseBVCookie=YES&animal=Puppet&oid=-12422</span></div><div class="shot live"><div class="ld">Loading the archived page from the Internet Archive…</div><iframe data-src="https://web.archive.org/web/20000301053925if_/http://www.pets.com:80/cgi-bin/puppy/products/category_home.jsp?BV_UseBVCookie=YES&animal=Puppet&oid=-12422" title="Archived http://www.pets.com/cgi-bin/puppy/products/category_home.jsp?BV_UseBVCookie=YES&animal=Puppet&oid=-12422 captured Mar 1, 2000 · 05:39 UTC" sandbox="allow-same-origin" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"></iframe></div></figure>
<figcaption class="wb-cap"><div class="d">Captured Mar 1, 2000 · 05:39 UTC</div><h3>Sock puppet gear</h3><p>The mascot had its own store category, “sock puppet gear”, alongside Dogs, Cats, Fish, Birds, Reptiles and Small Pets. Slate reported $20 puppets shipping in June 2000.</p><div class="arch"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20000301053925/http://www.pets.com:80/cgi-bin/puppy/products/category_home.jsp?BV_UseBVCookie=YES&animal=Puppet&oid=-12422" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View this capture on the Wayback Machine ↗</a></div></figcaption>
</div><div class="wb-view" data-i="5" hidden>
<figure class="browser"><div class="chrome"><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="url">http://www.pets.com/cgi-bin/puppy/animal/start.jsp?BV_UseBVCookie=YES&animal=Dogs&sourceid=00100694870504710715</span></div><div class="shot live"><div class="ld">Loading the archived page from the Internet Archive…</div><iframe data-src="https://web.archive.org/web/20000303164052if_/http://www.pets.com:80/cgi-bin/puppy/animal/start.jsp?BV_UseBVCookie=YES&animal=Dogs&sourceid=00100694870504710715" title="Archived http://www.pets.com/cgi-bin/puppy/animal/start.jsp?BV_UseBVCookie=YES&animal=Dogs&sourceid=00100694870504710715 captured Mar 3, 2000 · 16:40 UTC" sandbox="allow-same-origin" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"></iframe></div></figure>
<figcaption class="wb-cap"><div class="d">Captured Mar 3, 2000 · 16:40 UTC</div><h3>The dog store</h3><p>The dog department had dozens of categories, from beds to training aids. The S-1 claimed about 12,000 SKUs and planned more than 20,000 in 2000.</p><div class="arch"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20000303164052/http://www.pets.com:80/cgi-bin/puppy/animal/start.jsp?BV_UseBVCookie=YES&animal=Dogs&sourceid=00100694870504710715" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View this capture on the Wayback Machine ↗</a></div></figcaption>
</div><div class="wb-view" data-i="6" hidden>
<figure class="browser"><div class="chrome"><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="url">http://www.pets.com/</span></div><div class="shot live"><div class="ld">Loading the archived page from the Internet Archive…</div><iframe data-src="https://web.archive.org/web/20000510050603if_/http://www.pets.com:80/" title="Archived http://www.pets.com/ captured May 10, 2000 · 05:06 UTC" sandbox="allow-same-origin" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"></iframe></div></figure>
<figcaption class="wb-cap"><div class="d">Captured May 10, 2000 · 05:06 UTC</div><h3>Homepage, editorial content</h3><p>By spring the homepage mixed commerce with magazine-style articles (“Reptile Romance”, “Doing It Like Rabbits”).</p><div class="warn">Note: Images were not archived for this capture. Only the text and layout survive.</div><div class="arch"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20000510050603/http://www.pets.com:80/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View this capture on the Wayback Machine ↗</a></div></figcaption>
</div><div class="wb-view" data-i="7" hidden>
<figure class="browser"><div class="chrome"><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="url">http://www.pets.com/cgi-bin/puppy/products/detail.jsp?BV_UseBVCookie=YES&oid=35137&animal=Dogs&parOid=-11415&ITEM_FLAVOR=Chopped+beef</span></div><div class="shot live"><div class="ld">Loading the archived page from the Internet Archive…</div><iframe data-src="https://web.archive.org/web/20000607200024if_/http://www.pets.com:80/cgi-bin/puppy/products/detail.jsp?BV_UseBVCookie=YES&oid=35137&animal=Dogs&parOid=-11415&ITEM_FLAVOR=Chopped+beef" title="Archived http://www.pets.com/cgi-bin/puppy/products/detail.jsp?BV_UseBVCookie=YES&oid=35137&animal=Dogs&parOid=-11415&ITEM_FLAVOR=Chopped+beef captured Jun 7, 2000 · 20:00 UTC" sandbox="allow-same-origin" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"></iframe></div></figure>
<figcaption class="wb-cap"><div class="d">Captured Jun 7, 2000 · 20:00 UTC</div><h3>Product page: Pedigree canned</h3><p>A real product page: Pedigree canned dog food (manufacturer: Kal Kan) at “Our price: $15.98 – $21.98” against a “Retail” price of $17.99 – $23.99, in seven flavors and quantities of 1–6.</p><div class="warn">Note: Partial render: the product photo and some buttons are missing.</div><div class="arch"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20000607200024/http://www.pets.com:80/cgi-bin/puppy/products/detail.jsp?BV_UseBVCookie=YES&oid=35137&animal=Dogs&parOid=-11415&ITEM_FLAVOR=Chopped+beef" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View this capture on the Wayback Machine ↗</a></div></figcaption>
</div><div class="wb-view" data-i="8" hidden>
<figure class="browser"><div class="chrome"><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="url">http://pets.com/</span></div><div class="shot live"><div class="ld">Loading the archived page from the Internet Archive…</div><iframe data-src="https://web.archive.org/web/20001110064700if_/http://pets.com:80/" title="Archived http://pets.com/ captured Nov 10, 2000 · 06:47 UTC" sandbox="allow-same-origin" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"></iframe></div></figure>
<figcaption class="wb-cap"><div class="d">Captured Nov 10, 2000 · 06:47 UTC</div><h3>The farewell letter</h3><p>The day after the shutdown, the homepage was replaced by a letter: “We have closed our virtual doors effective November 9, 2000.”</p><div class="warn">Note: Cropped to the letter. The rest of the page is blank.</div><div class="arch"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20001110064700/http://pets.com:80/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View this capture on the Wayback Machine ↗</a></div></figcaption>
</div><div class="wb-view" data-i="9" hidden>
<figure class="browser"><div class="chrome"><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="url">http://pets.com/</span></div><div class="shot live"><div class="ld">Loading the archived page from the Internet Archive…</div><iframe data-src="https://web.archive.org/web/20010202130700if_/http://pets.com:80/" title="Archived http://pets.com/ captured Feb 2, 2001 · 13:07 UTC" sandbox="allow-same-origin" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"></iframe></div></figure>
<figcaption class="wb-cap"><div class="d">Captured Feb 2, 2001 · 13:07 UTC</div><h3>The new owner</h3><p>Three months later, pets.com redirected visitors to PETsMART.com: “Welcome Pets.com Customers! PETsMART.com has purchased the www.pets.com URL.”</p><div class="warn">Note: Some images are missing in the archived render.</div><div class="arch"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20010202130700/http://pets.com:80/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View this capture on the Wayback Machine ↗</a></div></figcaption>
</div></div>
<div class="snips">
<div class="snip"><b>Booda dog chews</b><br>Retail Price: $11.29–$13.69<br><b class="price">Our Price: $8.29</b><div class="when">www2.pets.com homepage · May 8, 1999<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-36">36</a></sup></div></div>
<div class="snip"><b>Nutro MAX Mini Chunk</b><br>Regular $26.49 · <b class="price">Sale $19.99</b><br><b>Cat gift basket</b> $29.99 → <b class="price">$18.99</b><div class="when">Holiday homepage · Nov 28, 1999<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-36">36</a></sup></div></div>
<div class="snip"><b>Pedigree canned dog food</b><br>Retail: $17.99–$23.99<br><b class="price">Our price: $15.98–$21.98</b><div class="when">Product page · Jun 7, 2000<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-36">36</a></sup></div></div>
<div class="snip">“We have closed our virtual doors effective November 9, 2000.… Orders placed before 11am PT on November 9 will be shipped.”<div class="when">Farewell letter · Nov 10, 2000 capture<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-36">36</a></sup></div></div>
<div class="snip">“Welcome Pets.com Customers! PETsMART.com has purchased the www.pets.com URL.” The welcome coupon’s $25 minimum excluded dog food, cat food and cat litter.<div class="when">pets.com · Feb 2, 2001 capture<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-36">36</a></sup></div></div>
<div class="snip">“Pets.com is a division of WebMagic™… headed by Greg McLemore.”<div class="when">About Us · May 8, 1999<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-36">36</a></sup></div></div>
</div>
<p class="note rv" style="margin-top:22px;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13.5px;color:var(--mute)">Read the prices against the economics in Section 04. The site routinely priced below “retail” on heavy staples such as canned and dry food. The new owner’s coupon left out exactly the heavy, low-margin staples Pets.com had shipped.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="economics" class="alt">
<div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-k"><span class="sn">04</span>Unit economics</div><h2 class="rv">Every box lost money</h2><p class="lede rv">Pet food is heavy, bulky and cheap per pound. Customers could buy it at any supermarket. Shipping it to them one order at a time was the problem the company never solved.</p></div>
<div class="g2">
<div class="card rv">
<div class="card-head"><div><h3>What $100 of sales cost to deliver</h3><p>Costs per $100 of revenue: the prospectus description, the prospectus target, and reported quarterly actuals.</p></div>
<div class="seg" id="econSeg"><button class="on" data-v="gross">Goods + shipping</button><button data-v="all">+ Marketing</button></div></div>
<div class="cbox tall"><canvas id="econChart"></canvas></div>
<div class="src">S-1 rows use the midpoints of stated ranges: product margins of 20–25% (product cost 75–80) and shipping costs of 100–110% of revenue, expected to fall to 20–25% once the Indianapolis center was at target volume<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup>. Actual rows are cost of goods sold and marketing &amp; sales ÷ net sales from the 10-Qs<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-4">4</a></sup>. In the filings, cost of goods sold includes outbound shipping. The dashed line marks $100, the break-even point.</div>
</div>
<div class="card rv">
<div class="card-head"><div><h3>Model: one hypothetical order</h3><p>Company-wide quarterly ratios applied to a single order. <b>This is an illustration, not order-level data.</b></p></div>
<div class="seg" id="calcSeg"><button class="on" data-v="q4">Q4 ’99</button><button data-v="q3">Q3 ’00</button><button data-v="tgt">S-1 target</button></div></div>
<div class="calc">
<div><label style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13px;color:var(--mute)">Order value: <b id="calcV" style="color:var(--ink)">$40</b></label><input type="range" id="calcR" min="15" max="150" step="5" value="40">
<div class="out" id="calcOut">−$287</div><div style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13px;color:var(--mute);margin-top:6px" id="calcNote">profit (loss) per order after marketing</div></div>
<div id="calcRows"></div>
</div>
